My original problem was

> I sucessfully installed a new Seagate disk in that I can mount it, write to
> it etc. as normal.  I put the new device into the /etc/fstab and it mounts
> on reboot also.
> 
> The strange thing is though that that the log for boot up does not show the
> new disk at scsi 1, here is the log:
> 
> scsi0 at tcds0 slot 0
>                                         rz2 at scsi0 bus 0 target 2 lun 0 (DEC
>                                          _    RZ28     (C) DEC D41C)
>                                         rz3 at scsi0 bus 0 target 3 lun 0 (DEC
>                                          _    RZ25L    (C) DEC 0007)
>                                         rz4 at scsi0 bus 0 target 4 lun 0 (DEC
>                                          _    RRD43   (C) DEC  1084)
>                                         rz6 at scsi0 bus 0 target 6 lun 0 (DEC
>                                          _    RX26     (C) DEC 0070)
>                                         tz5 at scsi0 bus 0 target 5 lun 0 (DEC
>                                          _    TLZ06     (C)DEC 491A)
> 
> There is no rz1 device listed!
> 
> The new disk is a Seagate Hawk 2  ST32151N.
> 
> Can anyone explain this?

The problem is that the version of Digital Unix I am using (3.2x) is too old
to automatically add the new disk information, even though the disk
functions normally.

To rectify this you need to rebuild the kernal by doing:

boot genvmunix
and build a new kernel with doconfig -c MYMACHINE

The new kernel will then recognize the new device.

