Hi,

Here is a email I sent to another person having the same problem, it details
what I did to get it to work. First you should check about telneting to the
card by doing the following, if you get a telnet connection then it should 
work:

# telnet 'printer JetDirect1' 9100
Trying ??.??.??.??...
Connected to JetDirect1.
Escape character is '^]'.

Here is the solutuion I have:

>> Joe,
>> 
>> Two questions for you - 
>> 1) How do you set up the telnet printing to be functional
>> 2) How/where do I get a firmware update???????

Question 2)

There is a HP Web page you go to where you can find and get firmware updates
etc. Its at "http://www.hp.com/", then go to the link "Support Documentation
and Software for Hewlett-Packard PC and Peripheral Products." and look for
'printer' stuff.

Question 1)

This ones a little harder. If you have the latest JetDirect card it should
already come with firmware that understands 'lpd'. I have got the J2382B-EX
JetDirect which is an external version. This is a earlier model, it does not
understand 'lpd'. You should be able to find specs on your model at the web 
page
which lists what it is capable of etc. But to get my model to understand 'lpd'
I first have to buy a 'Flash ROM Card' which inserts into the JetDirect card
and then download the latest firmware into it.

Also on the WEB Page, you will find available software called "HP JetAdmin"
which comes for a lot of systems but NOT for Digital Unix. In this batch of
software there is several programs you need to be able to print directly
to the JetDirect card using TELNET. Obviously its unusable for Digital Unix.
After wrangling between DEC & HP Technical support, I managed to get the 
source
code for these bits of software. I have included it at the end of this mail
uuencoded-zipped-tar file.

Here are the steps I used to install the software and get it to print to the
printer. NOTE: This uses 'lpr' to print to the printer, but when you do send
a file you can not see anything spooled using 'lpq' because each print job
runs this software as a deamon which talks directly to the JetDirect. This is
not a problem as the deamon just waits around untill it can print. Several of
these can be running at once.

Step 1)

Setup 'bootpd' so that the printer can get its IP address. You probably have
done this already, if not let me know and Ill give a short description how to.

Step 2)

Compile the software given at end of email. I made the following directly:
'/usr/var/spool/hpfilters' and untared the software there. Then just type
'make' and it should compile it.

Step 3) Use 'lprsetup' to make a printcap entry and setup a 'spool' directly
for it, add a 'output' filter as given in my printcap below. This is so 'lpr'
executes the script file 'hplj.of.sh'. You can put this anyware really:

#
# Print to HP LJ4MP via JetDirect box - use telnet to connect to box and
# transfer files
#
lp|JetDirect1|lp2:\
        :mx#0:\
        :lp=/dev/null:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd2:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lp2err:\
        :of=/usr/spool/lpd2/hplj.of.sh:\
        :sh:

Step 4) Create the 'hplj.of.sh' file in the directory you specified, the file
is given below:

- - - - - --------------->>>>>> Cut here 
<<<<<--------------------------------------------
#!/bin/sh
#
# Output filter script to print to a JetDirectEX box which has a LaserJet4MP
# connected to it - for Postscript & Raw ascii files
#
# Set the PATH variable so it can find the software you compiled
#
PATH="/usr/spool/hpfilters:$PATH"

#
# This sets up internal variables
#
SCAN=/usr/spool/hpfilters/hpnps
TMPFILE=".out.$$"

#
# Remove the last 'temp' file
#
trap "rm -f $TMPFILE;  exit 0;" 2

#
# This runs the 'SCAN' program which just determines whether the file is
# Postscript or Ascii - It reads from standard input the file been printed
# and then puts the file to be printed into a unique temp file - this allows
# more than one print job to occur
#
$SCAN > $TMPFILE
PERS=$?
if [ "$PERS" -eq 1 ]
then
	#
	# Here we print Postscript (ie: -P)
	#
        cat $TMPFILE | hpnpf -P -x JetDirect1 -p 9100 -s status > /dev/null
        rm -f $TMPFILE
else
	#
	# Here we print Ascii
	#
        cat $TMPFILE | hpnpf -n -x JetDirect1 -p 9100 -s status > /dev/null
        rm -f $TMPFILE
fi

exit $?

- - - - - --------------->>>> Cut here 
<<<<-----------------------------------------------

Step 5) With the programs compiled and this output filter file in place
you can print to the printer using 'lpr -P?? file'. There is also man pages
with the software, these do not list all that can be done by the software,
there is also lots of desription in the source code.

Hope this solves any problems you have.

Regards Joe
